ot only in the  sagittal plane but also in the frontal  plane of the transverse plane and this  is absolutely huge like i say it allows  you to adhere far more closely to the  principle of specificity so if you're  interested in functional training it's  probably because you want to get better  either at the things you do in your  day-to-day life or on a specific sport  and when it comes to a specific sport  then you're going to do things like  throwing a ball kicking a ball you might  be wrestling somebody in all these  scenarios you're actually exerting  strength forwards not up it's all in the  horizontal plane so having resistance  that's coming from down below is not  particularly useful and in regular life  you don't only exert strength by picking  things up you might be pushing furniture  you might be dragging something you  might be wrestling with a friend sure  you sometimes pick up your kid but even  if you do that you're probably going to  walk with them the best way to train  someone to swing a bat wouldn't be to  give them a heavier bat it would be to  provide resistance behind the bat so  they're swinging through  that resistance and at the same time  they're still using the exact same  trajectory and they're not pulling up  and down even something like a medicine  ball is a fantastic tool for so many  things but if you're just interested to  be able to throw further then that  weight is still the added weight is  still coming downwards and so you're not  really practicing the right kind of  thing and for all these reasons being  able to manipulate the angle of  resistance with bands with cables is  absolutely essential another big  advantage when it comes to training  specificity and trying to mimic a  specific movement is the option to add  different attachments to the resistance  bands in this case i've got a  grip that's supposed to be used for  pull-ups that strengthens your grip so  this is obviously much more difficult to  hold on to than something that is a  handle that wraps around your hand and  therefore i'm training my grip at the  same time as training my  core and my lats or my pecs whatever i'm  working  so if i wanted to become better at tug  of war because for some reason i keep  going on about that as an example then  pulling this is closer to pulling a rope  and of course i could just add a rope to  it and literally be practicing tug of  war should i do it slow motion  so here i'm going to be doing a single  arm band press and what's essentially  mimics is something like a bench press  it's a peck and shoulder and tricep  exercise where you're pushing forwards  against resistance the difference is  that when you perform a bench press  you're lying flat on your back what this  essentially does is it isolates the  chest it removes the role of the core in  stabilizing your body this is a really  good thing if you're interested in  building just massive pecs loads of  strength from the pecs and you want to  get a really big bench press however it  isn't that useful in terms of real world  transfer and of course the reason for  this is that whether you're helping a  friend move house and pushing furniture  whether you're wrestling somebody and  trying to push them over or trying to  push down a tree i don't know why  you don't have the luxury of lying flat  on your back and removing the roll of  the core in other words if you're not  training your core at the same time as  your chest then you're effectively  building your chest and not being able  to use it and jc santana showed me some  research that was conducted at his  institute of human performance where  they looked at the muscle activation of  the band press versus the chest press or  bench press and of course they're  completely different whereas one showed  massive activation in the pecs and  shoulders and triceps the other one  showed lesser activation there but also  much more activation in the core even  some in the legs and jc santana  