e we do so  much in the transverse plane especially  in sports just being the rotational  plane anything that involves twisting  whether you're throwing a baseball or  any other kind of ball whether you're  swinging a bat whether you're throwing a  punch you can exert so much power this  way this taps into the serrape effect  which combines some of the biggest  muscles that go across either side of  your body your rhomboids your obliques  and allows you to twist and throw at the  same time thereby really getting your  weight and power behind a movement it's  why a baseball pitcher is able to throw  balls at you know 100 miles an hour it  seems insane that our body can generate  that kind of power but we need to be  moving in this way to do it and using  bands whether it's a wood chopper  movement or whether it's pulling it down  from overhead is a fantastic way to  actually train this and to learn to  exert that kind of power from your body  and the obvious movement that a cable or  band punch out mimics is the punch and  there you can once again  add resistance to a punching movement  which is so much better than punching  holding weights which adds resistance  going downwards the endless uses of  wristbands just wrap the band around  the band and then hold on to it that way  it don't cut into your hand anymore  and now with this lighter resistance  band it's far more well suited to  practicing cable punch-outs or band  punch-outs in this case pulling the  bands there like that with the optional  rotation once again mimics the way that  we use our bodies in real life if we're  doing a tug of war pulling open a heavy  door or pulling back our hand after a  punch and training like this  pulling your arm back against resistance  can help you to snap back those punches  faster which not only is important for  making the technique look crisp and  preventing your arm from getting trapped  or something it also helps you to throw  the other arm out faster this is one of  t